\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Three ways to make a transformer block narrow, all drawn at the same total width $D$. Projecting into a narrow block costs a $W_{\mathrm  {down}}$/$W_{\mathrm  {up}}$ wrapper on \emph  {every} block. Partitioning costs nothing, because each block sits directly on its own slice of the stream and the $N$ slices concatenated are the representation. The single coupling that replaces the wrappers is detailed in Figure~\ref {fig:arch}.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:threeways}{{1}{2}{Three ways to make a transformer block narrow, all drawn at the same total width $\dmodel $. Projecting into a narrow block costs a $W_{\mathrm {down}}$/$W_{\mathrm {up}}$ wrapper on \emph {every} block. Partitioning costs nothing, because each block sits directly on its own slice of the stream and the $\nsub $ slices concatenated are the representation. The single coupling that replaces the wrappers is detailed in Figure~\ref {fig:arch}}{figure.1}{}}
